For any rational number $p/q$, numeration in base $p/q$ is defined by a function mapping finite words over the alphabet $A_p=\{0,1,...,p-1\}$ to rational values. The image of this function is denoted by $N_{p/q}$. While $N_{p/q}$ encompasses all nonnegative integers, existing literature typically concentrates on the subset of words evaluating to these integers; this set forms a chaotic, non-context-free language. In contrast, this work examines subsets of $(N_{p/q})^d$ that are $p/q$-recognisable, meaning they can be recognized by finite automata operating over $(A_p)^d$. First, we provide a characterization of these sets as those definable within a specific first-order logic, drawing a parallel to the B\"uchi-Bruy\`ere Theorem for integer-based numeration systems. Second, we demonstrate that neither the natural ordering relation nor the modulo-$q$ operator are $p/q$-recognisable. Finally, we offer a positive characterization of finite sets in the form of an Ehrenfeucht-Friedgut game and an accompanying table. These results establish that first-order B\"uchi recognisability in base $p/q$ does not necessarily imply recognisability in the set of integers, a phenomenon that also occurs in real-Arithmetic B\"uchi recognisability. The concepts of $p/q$-recognisability and the associated automata find applications across number theory, computer science, and linguistics, including in the study of $p/q$-integers, the B\"uchi-Bruy\`ere Theorem for such integers, and the analysis of formal languages composed of $p/q$-integers. Key terms: $p/q$-integers, $p/q$-numerations, finite automata, B\"uchi automata, B\"uchi recognisable sets, $p$-integers, $p$-numerations, integer automata, real automata, real-B\"uchi recognisable sets, and B\"uchi-Bruy\`ere Theorem.
